WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.670
(chuckles)
- Oh, this is gonna be great.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.297
- Are you watching old Two Cents videos?

00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:08.842
- Yeah, I just learned
that we can make a bundle

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:12.303
by turning our creative content into NFTs.

00:00:12.303 --> 00:00:14.514
I'm gonna make us crypto millionaires

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.599
by turning our catchphrases into gold.

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:20.103
I bet we could sell a Run the Numbers NFT.

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:22.272
Ooh, maybe I could turn
my old mustache into one.

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:26.109
Oh, those are like rainbow
cat Bitcoins, right?

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:28.570
But how are you gonna
make money off of that?

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.614
- Well, I mean, I don't
really understand it,

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:33.241
but the way my friend explained it to me,

00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:35.702
first, you mine a piece of art,

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:38.121
and then something, something, something,

00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:40.999
until one day you just cash
out and live in The Bahamas.

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:42.792
- Oh, that sounds legit, huh.

00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:45.670
- Ooh, I wonder if we could
turn our dog into an NFT.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.589
She has not been pulling
her financial weight

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:48.798
around here lately.

00:00:48.798 --> 00:00:49.632
- Oh.

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:52.135
(playful music)

00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:01.394
By this point, you've
likely seen the letters NFT

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:05.273
drift across evening news,
or even your TikTok feed.

00:01:05.273 --> 00:01:09.319
NFTs, or non-fungible
tokens, are a new way

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:13.198
to own unique digital assets
through the blockchain.

00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:15.325
They've actually been around since 2014

00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:18.578
but have exploded in
popularity in recent years.

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:20.163
- To quote Julie Andrews,

00:01:20.163 --> 00:01:22.123
"Let's start at the very beginning."

00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:24.459
If I wanted to create an NFT,

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:27.504
I need to begin by creating
two online accounts,

00:01:27.504 --> 00:01:29.130
a cryptocurrency wallet

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.550
that allows me to hold
cryptocurrency, like Coinbase,

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.637
and an NFT marketplace
account like OpenSea.

00:01:35.637 --> 00:01:37.430
Once I've created a profile

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.891
and verified my credentials
on these platforms,

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:42.685
the next step is to
decide on a digital asset

00:01:42.685 --> 00:01:44.896
I wish to turn into an NFT.

00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:47.107
I've decided this sweet picture

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:49.192
of our dog jazz should fit the bill.

00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:51.236
- Philip will then upload the image

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:53.696
to the marketplace and click Create.

00:01:53.696 --> 00:01:56.407
When he does this, the platform
will permanently record

00:01:56.407 --> 00:01:58.785
onto a blockchain like Ethereum

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:02.372
that Philip's account
owns this digital work.

00:02:02.372 --> 00:02:04.290
To create this entry, he'll have to pay

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.001
what are called gas
fees to make the record.

00:02:07.001 --> 00:02:09.671
He will of course have
to pay them with Ethereum

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:13.258
and it will cost him the
equivalent of 10 to 100 bucks.

00:02:13.258 --> 00:02:16.886
Once the ownership of this
entry on the blockchain is made,

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:19.764
Phillip's account will
receive a digital token

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:22.559
proving his ownership that's
unique to his account.

00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:25.603
- This token is non-fungible,

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:28.106
meaning that there is
no other token like it

00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:29.941
and it cannot be duplicated.

00:02:29.941 --> 00:02:31.192
So now what?

00:02:31.192 --> 00:02:33.653
I can just keep it if I so choose,

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.197
or I could sell it outright
or list it for auction

00:02:36.197 --> 00:02:37.615
if I thought multiple people

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:39.492
might be interested in owning it.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.995
People might even get
into a bidding war for it.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:45.123
I mean, come on, she's the
goodest, cutest girl ever.

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:47.250
- Let's say his hunch is right.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.377
And there turns out to be a bidding war,

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:52.505
and it sells for $200 worth of Ethereum.

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:55.508
The new owner now has the NFT

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:57.635
that is recorded on the blockchain,

00:02:57.635 --> 00:03:01.514
proving to everyone that
they own this digital asset.

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:04.726
but it's not like they own copyright.

00:03:04.726 --> 00:03:08.021
Phillip still has this image on his phone,

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:09.647
and anyone else could come along

00:03:09.647 --> 00:03:11.566
and snap a screenshot of the image

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.443
and it would be perfectly legal.

00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:17.155
So why would anyone wanna
bid on something like this?

00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:19.073
- At this stage in their evolution,

00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:22.327
NFTs primarily represent creative works,

00:03:22.327 --> 00:03:25.622
such as visual art, music,
and video game items.

00:03:25.622 --> 00:03:29.000
The appeal to own such
items is no different

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.379
than wanting to own an original
Picasso rather than a print.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:34.631
It's a flex.

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:37.634
And since NFTs are displayed publicly,

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:39.761
you can show off far and wide,

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:41.846
not just to people who
come into your house.

00:03:41.846 --> 00:03:43.765
And like an original Picasso,

00:03:43.765 --> 00:03:46.893
if you have a piece that's
special or rare enough,

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.979
it could turn into a lucrative investment.

00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:53.149
- The very first NFT was
for a digital art piece

00:03:53.149 --> 00:03:55.568
by Kevin McCoy called Quantum.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:59.822
And it recently sold at
Sotheby's for $1.4 million.

00:03:59.822 --> 00:04:00.949
That's right.

00:04:00.949 --> 00:04:05.328
Someone paid 1.4 mil for
the right to claim ownership

00:04:05.328 --> 00:04:07.163
over a pixel art piece

00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:09.999
that anyone can take
a video screenshot of.

00:04:09.999 --> 00:04:12.460
Jack Dorsey, a co-founder of Twitter,

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.756
sold an NFT for his very
first tweet for $2.9 million.

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:20.635
And in 2021, the digital artist Beeple

00:04:20.635 --> 00:04:25.014
sold a collection of
his work for $69 million

00:04:25.014 --> 00:04:26.891
at Christie's Auction.

00:04:26.891 --> 00:04:28.017
No doubt about it.

00:04:28.017 --> 00:04:31.479
Serious money is rapidly
flowing into this market

00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:33.314
and it's turning lots of heads.

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:35.942
- To many of us, this might still feel

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:38.444
like a digital version of Beanie Babies,

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.280
but most advocates believe

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.407
that we're merely witnessing NFTs

00:04:42.407 --> 00:04:45.034
in the first phase of their evolution.

00:04:45.034 --> 00:04:48.329
Remember, an NFT is not a piece of art,

00:04:48.329 --> 00:04:52.166
it's inalterable proof of
ownership of what, for now,

00:04:52.166 --> 00:04:53.793
is mostly creative work.

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:56.963
But NFTs could easily
be used in the future

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.757
to represent the ownership
of a piece of real estate

00:04:59.757 --> 00:05:02.093
or perhaps you might receive even NFT

00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:05.430
upon graduating college to
show ownership of your degree.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:09.475
- NFTs promise to empower
a new creator economy,

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:12.937
where creators don't hand
over ownership of their work

00:05:12.937 --> 00:05:15.064
to a publishing or streaming platform.

00:05:15.064 --> 00:05:18.776
Ownership is baked into
the content itself.

00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:21.988
along with any royalties or
future income from the work.

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:25.575
For example, what if Beyonce's next album

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:28.619
was sold by creating 20,000 NFTs?

00:05:28.619 --> 00:05:31.289
And the only way to access the music

00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:33.791
was by providing your proof of ownership.

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:36.419
I know I'd be setting
up a Coinbase account

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:38.713
quicker than you could say blue ivy.

00:05:38.713 --> 00:05:41.132
- But again, this is the
future we're talking about.

00:05:41.132 --> 00:05:43.885
What if you're getting
pressured by your roommate

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:47.680
to dump a bunch of money into
Bored Ape NFTs right now?

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.892
Is it a bubble that you
should stay far away from?

00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:53.478
Or is your FOMO justified?

00:05:53.478 --> 00:05:55.980
- In our opinion, neither.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.316
Sure, the piles of money and buzz

00:05:58.316 --> 00:06:02.028
pouring into NFTs right now
does look a lot like a bubble.

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:03.404
And it likely is.

00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:06.407
But many new technologies often begin

00:06:06.407 --> 00:06:08.284
with a bubble-like stage

00:06:08.284 --> 00:06:11.162
that actually ends up
pushing innovation forward.

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:14.123
Think about the .com boom and bust,

00:06:14.123 --> 00:06:17.877
or even the bicycle bubble
in Britain in the 1890s.

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.671
Oh yeah, that actually happened.

00:06:20.671 --> 00:06:23.132
But thankfully, bikes and the internet

00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:24.926
are still very much a thing.

00:06:24.926 --> 00:06:27.804
We are in the early
days of cryptocurrency,

00:06:27.804 --> 00:06:30.014
blockchain use and NFTs,

00:06:30.014 --> 00:06:31.933
which could be a good time

00:06:31.933 --> 00:06:34.102
to start to dip your
toes in the water, right?

00:06:34.102 --> 00:06:36.771
- Perhaps, but here's a word of caution.

00:06:36.771 --> 00:06:39.816
If you're looking to NFTs
as a way to get rich,

00:06:39.816 --> 00:06:41.484
you might want to reassess.

00:06:41.484 --> 00:06:44.487
We never recommend engaging
in speculative investments

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:46.781
with money you can't afford to lose.

00:06:46.781 --> 00:06:48.449
And it's never a good idea to invest

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:50.993
in anything you don't really understand.

00:06:50.993 --> 00:06:52.245
- If you've done your research

00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:55.123
and are ready to take a
bit of a gamble, go for it.

00:06:55.123 --> 00:06:57.291
Just make sure you're
keeping the percentage

00:06:57.291 --> 00:07:00.128
in emerging asset classes like this low

00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:01.629
in your overall portfolio.

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:04.382
- So how do you think
NFTs will shape our world

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:05.508
in the coming years?

00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:08.261
Will this be a flash in the pan after all,

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:09.887
or the future of ownership?

00:07:09.887 --> 00:07:12.557
We'll just have to wait an NF-see.

